.TH cp "" "Command"
.SH NAME
cp \- copy files
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBcp \fIoldfile newfile\fR
.br
\fBcp \fIfile ... directory\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.HS
cp -- copy files
Usage:	cp oldfile newfile
	cp file ... directory
.HE
.B cp
copies
.I oldfile
to
.IR newfile ,
creating
.I newfile
if necessary.
.PP
In the second form, if the last argument is an existing directory,
.B cp
copies each
.I file
into that directory, keeping the same base name.
.PP
The new file's contents are a byte-for-byte copy of the source.
Blocks of the source that are entirely zero are reproduced by
seeking over them in the new file (preserving holes in a sparse
file) rather than by writing zero bytes, except that the last block
written always contains real data so the copy has the correct
length.
The new file's permission bits are copied from the source's low 9
mode bits; the setuid, setgid, and sticky bits are not copied, and
the new file is owned by the user running
.BR cp .
.PP
A file may not be copied onto itself.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR cpdir ,
.BR ln ,
.B mv
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.B cp
prints a message and skips a file if it is a directory, cannot be
opened or created, is the same file as the destination, or is a
busy shared-text file that cannot be overwritten; a write error also
aborts that file's copy.
.SH NOTES
When copying multiple files into a directory, the exit status
reflects only whether at least one file was copied successfully:
.B cp
exits 0 if any copy succeeded, and 1 only if every copy failed.
